<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" ?>
<!--
  The graded ladder: what a pawn feels about another pawn purely because of where
  their two Colors sit.

  WHY THIS IS NOT A PreferredXenotype PRECEPT

  Vanilla's is a flat three-way switch - liked, disliked, neutral - capped at three
  preferred xenotypes. With fifteen Colors that cannot express the thing the
  setting is actually about, which is that the feeling is GRADED and SIGNED: a
  Silver looks down on a Red and up at a Gold, and the size of both depends on the
  distance between them. So the stage is chosen in
  Source/RimRising/ThoughtWorker_ColorRank.cs and this file is the belief it reads.

  THE RANK IS NOT DUPLICATED HERE. The worker reads XenotypeDef.displayPriority
  straight out of XenotypeDefs_Colors.xml, where the canonical order already lives -
  Gold 10 down to Red 140, with sub-strains beside their parents. Ten points is one
  rung. Reorder the Colors there and the ladder follows; there is no second list to
  keep in sync.

  THE STAGES BELOW ARE POSITIONAL. The worker indexes them, so their order and
  count must match StageUpperBound in that file.

  Structure mirrored from Biotech/Defs/PreceptDefs/Precepts_Bloodfeeders.xml, the
  vanilla example of a precept installing a situational SOCIAL thought.
-->
<Defs>

  <IssueDef>
    <defName>RR_ColorDeference</defName>
    <label>deference between Colors</label>
    <iconPath>UI/Issues/RR_ColorDeference</iconPath>
  </IssueDef>

  <!-- ====================== the hierarchy's own ladder ===================== -->
  <!--
    Note the asymmetry, which is deliberate and is the whole design.

    Looking DOWN is mildly sour: a Gold does not hate a Red, a Gold barely notices
    a Red, and contempt is cheap. Looking UP is strongly positive: the hierarchy
    survives because the people at the bottom believe in it, and awe is what does
    that work. The net effect in a mixed colony is that lowColors are content and
    highColors are faintly irritated - which is exactly the Society functioning as
    intended, and exactly what makes an egalitarian colony feel different.

    Same-rung kinship is positive because a caste is also a community.
  -->
  <ThoughtDef>
    <defName>RR_Thought_ColorRank</defName>
    <thoughtClass>Thought_SituationalSocial</thoughtClass>
    <workerClass>RimRising.ThoughtWorker_ColorRank</workerClass>
    <validWhileDespawned>true</validWhileDespawned>
    <stages>
      <li>
        <label>far beneath me</label>
        <description>Whatever this one is for, it is not for talking to.</description>
        <baseOpinionOffset>-20</baseOpinionOffset>
      </li>
      <li>
        <label>well beneath me</label>
        <description>Useful enough in its place. Its place is not here.</description>
        <baseOpinionOffset>-12</baseOpinionOffset>
      </li>
      <li>
        <label>beneath me</label>
        <description>Close enough to my station to be irritating about it.</description>
        <baseOpinionOffset>-5</baseOpinionOffset>
      </li>
      <li>
        <label>of my own Color</label>
        <description>Made for the same work, by the same hands, to the same specification. That counts for something.</description>
        <baseOpinionOffset>8</baseOpinionOffset>
      </li>
      <li>
        <label>above me</label>
        <description>Owed courtesy, and getting it.</description>
        <baseOpinionOffset>6</baseOpinionOffset>
      </li>
      <li>
        <label>well above me</label>
        <description>The sort of person decisions come down from.</description>
        <baseOpinionOffset>14</baseOpinionOffset>
      </li>
      <li>
        <label>far above me</label>
        <description>It is a privilege to be in the room. Everyone has always said so.</description>
        <baseOpinionOffset>22</baseOpinionOffset>
      </li>
    </stages>
  </ThoughtDef>

  <PreceptDef>
    <defName>RR_ColorDeference_Graded</defName>
    <issue>RR_ColorDeference</issue>
    <label>the ladder</label>
    <description>Every Color has a station, and the distance between two stations is a real quantity that both parties can feel. Deference upward is not servility, it is accuracy. Contempt downward is not cruelty, it is also accuracy.</description>
    <impact>High</impact>
    <displayOrderInIssue>10</displayOrderInIssue>
    <associatedMemes>
      <li>RR_ColorHierarchy</li>
      <li>RR_GoldWorship</li>
    </associatedMemes>
    <conflictingMemes>
      <li>RR_ColorEquality</li>
    </conflictingMemes>
    <comps>
      <li Class="PreceptComp_SituationalThought">
        <thought>RR_Thought_ColorRank</thought>
        <description>Another Color's station</description>
      </li>
    </comps>
  </PreceptDef>

  <!-- ========================== the other reading ========================== -->
  <!--
    Not merely the absence of the ladder. An egalitarian believer is actively
    pleased by company from across it, so RR_ColorEquality has something to do in
    daily play rather than only a goodwill number at the faction level.
  -->
  <ThoughtDef>
    <defName>RR_Thought_ColorCrossing</defName>
    <thoughtClass>Thought_SituationalSocial</thoughtClass>
    <workerClass>RimRising.ThoughtWorker_ColorRank_Equality</workerClass>
    <validWhileDespawned>true</validWhileDespawned>
    <stages>
      <li>
        <label>a different Color</label>
        <description>Someone the registries would have kept in another building entirely.</description>
        <baseOpinionOffset>8</baseOpinionOffset>
      </li>
      <li>
        <label>the whole ladder away</label>
        <description>Two people who by every rule of the Society should never have met, let alone eaten at the same table.</description>
        <baseOpinionOffset>18</baseOpinionOffset>
      </li>
    </stages>
  </ThoughtDef>

  <PreceptDef>
    <defName>RR_ColorDeference_None</defName>
    <issue>RR_ColorDeference</issue>
    <label>no ladder</label>
    <description>The Colors were an engineering decision made by people who are dead, about work most of us no longer do. Whatever a person was built for, what they are is the person standing in front of you.</description>
    <impact>High</impact>
    <displayOrderInIssue>20</displayOrderInIssue>
    <associatedMemes>
      <li>RR_ColorEquality</li>
    </associatedMemes>
    <conflictingMemes>
      <li>RR_ColorHierarchy</li>
      <li>RR_GoldWorship</li>
    </conflictingMemes>
    <comps>
      <li Class="PreceptComp_SituationalThought">
        <thought>RR_Thought_ColorCrossing</thought>
        <description>Company from across the Colors</description>
      </li>
    </comps>
  </PreceptDef>

</Defs>
